/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - IS 456 defines the characteristic strength of a material as the strength below which not more than 5 % of the test results are expected to fall.
   - Test results from one mix cluster around their mean μ, the average.
   - μ is the mean cube strength and σ the standard deviation, both in N/mm².
   - Why not use the mean?
   - A mix designer works the rule backwards.
   - s is the standard deviation expected on that site.
   - For M25 the target is 25 + 1.65 × 4 = 31.6 N/mm²; for M30 it is 30 + 1.65 × 5 = 38.25 N/mm².
   - For loads the unsafe direction is upward.
   - Qk is the characteristic load; μQ and σQ are in the same unit, kN or kN/m².
   - Enough statistics rarely exist for real loads, so IS 456 takes the loads of IS 875 (dead, imposed, wind, snow) and IS 1893 (earthquake) as the characteristic loads.
*/
