/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - IS 456 analyses a section at collapse in flexure with these assumptions: plane sections remain plane; the strain in the extreme compression fibre is 0.0035; the tensile strength of concrete is ignored; the stress in concrete follows the des
   - Let xu be the depth of the neutral axis at collapse.
   - Per unit width, the compression force is the area of the stress diagram, written in terms of the peak stress 0.446fck:
   - The parabola's area is two-thirds of the rectangle that encloses it.
   - The centroid of the rectangle is (3/14) xu below the top.
   - So C = 0.36fck b xu acts at 0.42xu below the top, and the lever arm to the steel is z = d − 0.42xu.
   - The stress block is therefore not a uniform stress of 0.36fck; 0.36fck is the stress the force would have if it were spread evenly over xu, and 0.42 locates where it really acts.
   - b and d in mm, Ast in mm², fck and fy in N/mm²; Mu in N mm.
   - For a ductile failure the steel must reach 0.87fy before the concrete crushes, so its strain must be at least 0.87fy/Es + 0.002 when the top fibre is at 0.0035.
   - The last column is 0.36 (xu,max/d)(1 − 0.42 xu,max/d): for Fe415, 0.36 × 0.48 × (1 − 0.42 × 0.48) = 0.138.
*/
